<h1>About the data</h1>

<h2>Where it comes from</h2>
<p>Most US tax-exempt organizations file an annual information return with the IRS:
  Form 990 (larger organizations), Form 990-EZ (smaller ones) or Form 990-PF (private
  foundations). These returns are public. The IRS publishes the ones filed electronically
  as XML files on its
  <a href="https://www.irs.gov/charities-non-profits/form-990-series-downloads">Form 990 series downloads</a>
  page. This site reads every return in those files and keeps each organization's most
  recent one. The
  <a href="https://www.irs.gov/charities-non-profits/exempt-organizations-business-master-file-extract-eo-bmf">Exempt Organizations Business Master File</a>
  adds each organization's category (NTEE code).</p>

<h2>What the numbers mean</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Rank</strong>: by total revenue on the latest return. The top list and the
    pages cover organizations above a size threshold.</li>
  <li><strong>Salary base</strong>: Form 990 Part IX lines 5, 6 and 7, which is pay to officers and
    directors, to disqualified persons, and all other salaries and wages. Benefits, pensions
    and payroll taxes are shown separately as "salaries, other pay and benefits" (Part I line 15).
    For Form 990-EZ the IRS asks for one combined figure. For Form 990-PF it is officers' pay
    plus other employee salaries and wages.</li>
  <li><strong>Top 10 paid</strong>: the ten highest totals among the people the return lists
    in Form 990 Part VII. Totals add reportable pay from the organization, reportable pay from
    related organizations, and other pay (such as deferred pay and benefits). Organizations
    must list officers, directors and trustees, key employees, and their five highest paid
    employees earning over $100,000. Pay from related organizations does not come out of the
    salary base, so the "top 10 share" can overstate slightly when it is large.</li>
  <li><strong>Employees</strong>: the number of W-2s the organization filed for the calendar year,
    which counts part time and seasonal staff the same as full time.</li>
  <li><strong>Board members</strong>: people marked as an individual or institutional trustee or
    director. On Form 990-EZ and 990-PF there is no such mark, so this site reads the title.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Matching people across organizations</h2>
<p>Returns list people by name only. This site joins names after removing titles,
  suffixes, middle names and initials, so "Dr. Jane Q. Example, PhD" and "JANE EXAMPLE" are
  the same page. Different people with the same name are joined too. Every person page says so.</p>

<h2>Limits</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Only electronically filed returns are included. Almost all large organizations e-file,
    as the law has required since tax years beginning after July 1, 2019.</li>
  <li>Churches and some religious organizations do not have to file, and do not appear.</li>
  <li>Hospitals, universities and others often split into several related organizations,
    each with its own return. Each is ranked on its own.</li>
  <li>Figures are as filed and are not audited or corrected here.</li>
</ul>
